{"hash":"e24e82f0dd301f93","voice":"en-IN-NeerjaExpressiveNeural","rate":"+4%","dur":82.103,"said":"Om Namah Shivaya. Come, walk with me into Srisailam. We have climbed high into the Nallamala hills, thick with forest, with the river Krishna winding far below. The Puranas call this place Bhukailasa, Kailasa on earth, and the temple trust says honestly that no record survives of how it all began. Above you rises the great eastern gopuram, one of four gateways in the temple’s tall stone walls. Kings and queens have climbed these hills before you. More than six hundred years ago, Vittalamba, a queen of the Vijayanagara empire, had a long flight of stone steps built down to the river at Patalaganga. Her husband, King Harihara the Second, gave the pillared hall that stands before the sanctum; we will rest there later. And in 1677 the great Maratha king, Chhatrapati Shivaji Maharaj, came here as a pilgrim; the northern gateway is remembered as his gift. But what drew kings and queens, and countless ordinary families, up into these forests? The answer is carved on the walls ahead. Walk with me.\n\nDid you know? The temple’s outer wall encloses an area of about 183 by 152 metres and stands about 8.5 metres high.","segments":[{"key":"text","start":0,"end":1004},{"key":"didYouKnow","start":1020,"end":1121}],"words":[[0.098,0.204,0,2],[0.314,0.312,3,8],[0.639,0.553,9,16],[2.127,0.433,18,22],[2.764,0.312,24,28],[3.089,0.216,29,33],[3.317,0.156,34,36],[3.486,0.264,37,41],[3.762,0.769,42,51],[5.457,0.168,53,55],[5.637,0.168,56,60],[5.817,0.409,61,68],[6.238,0.312,69,73],[6.562,0.252,74,78],[6.827,0.072,79,82],[6.911,0.505,83,92],[7.428,0.421,93,98],[8.101,0.204,100,105],[8.317,0.168,106,110],[8.498,0.601,111,117],[9.291,0.12,119,123],[9.423,0.084,124,127],[9.519,0.373,128,133],[9.904,0.409,134,141],[10.325,0.409,142,149],[10.745,0.264,150,153],[11.022,0.385,154,159],[12.308,0.108,161,164],[12.428,0.589,165,172],[13.029,0.3,173,177],[13.341,0.168,178,182],[13.522,0.349,183,188],[1